===Etymology===
While the term is used in [[w:Song of Solomon|Song of Solomon]] 7:4, its meaning is [[literal]]. The current [[figurative]] meaning comes from {{etyl|fr}} [[tour d'ivoire]], attributed to [[w:Charles Augustin Sainte-Beuve|Charles Augustin Sainte-Beuve]], who used it in ''Les consolations'' poem to compare the poet [[w:Alfred de Vigny|Alfred de Vigny]] (more isolated) with [[w:Victor Hugo|Victor Hugo]] (more socially engaged)<ref>('''fr''') Joseph Delorme, ''Poésies complètes de Sainte-Beuve'', Charpentier et Cie, 1869, p. 374, [http://books.google.ca/books?id=7JMGAAAAQAAJ&ots=kA1hd18A7v&hl=en&pg=PA374&f=false#v=onepage&q&f=false « Les consolations »]</ref>. The first known written use in English is ''H. L. Bergson's Laughter'' (1911) by Frederick Rothwell and Cloudesley Shovell Henry Brereton.<ref>('''en''') The Phrase Finder [http://www.phrases.org.uk/meanings/210800.html "ivory tower"]</ref>

